From 54e66c3de5abadb0648fa03600f19e00a6264443 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: BellSoft <info@bell-sw.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 20:00:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(vendorjdk): detect standalone vendor JDK and NIK
 runtimes

The vendor's JDK and Native Image Kit are also shipped standalone (tarballs,
.deb/.rpm packages, runtimes copied into images of other distributions) and
carry no apk metadata. Such runtimes are now detected and matched against
the OSV ecosystems of the module's JDK and NIK families ("<family>:<major>"
and "<family>:<nik major>-<java major>") served by the vendor OSV source.

Detection is the shared one (github.com/bell-sw/osv-internal/vendorjdk):
anchored on libjvm.so, vendor and HotSpot banner from its string table,
corroborated by the release file (Java 9+) or the rt.jar manifest (Java
8). Other vendors are never reported. The post-analyzer in
pkg/fanal/analyzer/language/java/vendorjdk collects the files the detector
reads and emits one application per runtime with a single package: name =
variant (jdk, jre, jdk-lite, ... / NIK bundle core, standard, full),
product version (21.0.2+14, 8u402+7, 23.1.0+1), ecosystem in SrcName and
the module's purl
pkg:generic/<namespace>/<jdk or nik name>@<version>?package_type=<variant>.
The package types (ftypes.VendorJDK, VendorNIK) and their severity source
ids take their values from the module's purl names. A GraalVM-based runtime
is reported once, as NIK. macOS (libjvm.dylib) and Windows (jvm.dll)
layouts are collected as well.

Runtimes installed by apk on the vendor distributions live under
/usr/lib/jvm and are covered by the OS package scan, so they are dropped
from the language results on those two families.

The tests build their runtimes with the module's testfix package.
